    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "yeah that makes sense. one more thing—i've heard some people say that mutual funds can be more \"unpredictable\" in terms of how the fund manager handles volatility or unexpected market shifts. is there any way to describe those little inefficiencies or hidden technical glitches that can mess up the fund's performance? i want to make sure i explain to the client why things might not always go perfectly according to the prospectus.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The perceived unpredictability in mutual funds often relates to **manager style and decision-making**, not technical glitches. Active managers attempt to beat the market, but their specific stock picks or timing decisions can lead to higher volatility during market shifts. Hidden inefficiencies can arise from high trading turnover in an active fund, which generates substantial transaction costs, known as **slippage**, eroding returns. For passive ETFs, the performance deviation is usually just tracking error relative to the index, which is generally very small and predictable.",
      "source": "target",
